DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     These interfaces may be used to convert between the local ( errno(2)) and
     BSM error number spaces found in BSM return tokens.

     The aauu__bbssmm__ttoo__eerrrrnnoo() function accepts a BSM error value, _b_s_m___e_r_r_o_r, and
     converts it to an errno(2) that will be stored in the integer pointed to
     by _e_r_r_o_r_p if successful.  This call will fail if the BSM error cannot be
     mapped into a local error number, which may occur if the return token was
     generated on another operating system.

     The aauu__eerrrrnnoo__ttoo__bbssmm() function accepts a local errno(2) value, and re‐
     turns the BSM error number for it.  This call cannot fail, and instead
     returns a BSM error number indicating to a later decoder that the error
     could not be encoded.

     The aauu__ssttrreerrrroorr() function converts a BSM error value to a string, gener‐
     ally by converting first to a local error number and using the local
     strerror(3) function, but will also work for errors that are not locally
     defined.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     On success, aauu__bbssmm__ttoo__eerrrrnnoo() returns 0 and a converted error value; on
     failure, it returns -1 but does not set errno(2).

     On success, aauu__ssttrreerrrroorr() returns a pointer to an error string; on fail‐
     ure it will return NULL.

